WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
DNS maps names to services and publishes supporting information.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.230
An A record maps a name to an IPv4 address, while an AAAA record maps it to IPv6.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.345
A CNAME record aliases one name to another,

00:00:11.345 --> 00:00:14.600
often revealing a hosted platform or service dependency.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.565
MX records identify mail exchangers.

00:00:16.565 --> 00:00:19.315
NS records identify authoritative name servers.

00:00:19.315 --> 00:00:23.120
TXT records carry text used for purposes such as domain verification,

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.668
email-security policy, and service configuration.

00:00:25.668 --> 00:00:30.330
A TXT value should be interpreted according to its specific standard and context.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.190
Together, records can describe public service names, mail providers,

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:36.080
verification relationships, and third-party dependencies.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.625
They can also expose stale configurations or inconsistent ownership that deserves internal

00:00:40.625 --> 00:00:41.360
review.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.334
Interpret DNS cautiously.

00:00:42.334 --> 00:00:46.500
Records may be cached, changed, split by resolver location, fronted by a proxy,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.240
or delegated to a provider.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.075
One destination can serve many customers, and one service can use many addresses.

00:00:52.075 --> 00:00:54.085
A record is an observed configuration,

00:00:54.085 --> 00:00:57.640
not automatic proof of who operates the underlying system.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:00:58.640
Time matters.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.825
Note when the record was observed and whether the source represents current, historical,

00:01:01.825 --> 00:01:03.990
passive, or authoritative data.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.640
Independent sources with different collection paths can increase confidence,

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.510
but repeated copies of one record are not independent corroboration.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:13.870
Defensive analysis should reconcile public DNS with approved asset inventories,

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:15.450
service owners, and vendor records.

00:01:15.450 --> 00:01:18.580
The useful outcome is a documented question or remediation item,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.180
not an unsupported conclusion based on a single lookup.
